WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
With negligible external torque, contraction preserves angular momentum

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.520
while reducing moment of inertia. An idealized object

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.860
with unchanged mass and shape that halves its

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.960
radius spins four times faster. Its period becomes

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.040
one quarter as long. The familiar contracting skater

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.540
analogy explains the trend; it does not make

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.180
the actual Sun a neutron star.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.100
The magnetic axis can tilt away from the

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.920
rotation axis. As the neutron star rotates, its

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.660
directed emission sweeps around like a lighthouse beam.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.360
An observer receives pulses when that beam crosses

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.300
the line of sight. If the beam misses

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.360
Earth throughout the rotation, the star can keep

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.660
spinning without visible beam pulses here.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:40.650
A magnetar is a neutron star with an

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:44.530
exceptionally strong magnetic field and related activity. Observed

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.990
magnetars need not spin in milliseconds; some have

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:51.150
second long periods. A rapidly rotating newborn magnetar

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.090
can be modeled as an energy source for

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:56.330
some luminous supernovae. Keep that conditional birth model

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:58.510
separate from every magnetar’s present rotation.
